# ---- 815 Metal Fabrication and Machining I ----
H("815.d.1.A",3,"A fabrication apprentice reports a material shortage to the shop foreman verbally, then emails the details to the supplier and the project engineer. She is demonstrating effective oral and written communication with:","individuals from varied roles and backgrounds",["only her teammates","only her relatives","only the person she likes"],"Different audiences need clear messages.")
H("815.d.1.A",4,"An email to a supplier about a late steel delivery should state:","the order number, the item and the needed date",["only that the shop is upset","a joke about the delay","nothing specific"],"Specific details get a response.")
H("815.d.1.B",3,"A welder studies a print that shows a groove weld symbol, a root opening and a bevel angle before joining two plates. He is interpreting:","engineering drawings, charts, diagrams and welding symbols",["a travel map","a school schedule","a movie poster"],"Weld symbols convey weld type and size.")
H("815.d.1.B",4,"In a welding symbol, the weld type is shown by the symbol placed:","on the reference line",["on the tail only","outside the drawing","on the border"],"The symbol sits on the reference line.")
H("815.d.1.C",3,"To lay out a pattern for a cone, a fabricator uses geometry to find the arc length and angle of the flat shape that will roll into it. She is selecting:","algebraic and geometric principles for precision measurement and calculation",["only art supplies","only a guess","only a sports play"],"Geometry guides layout.")
H("815.d.1.C",4,"The circumference of a round pipe is found with the formula:","pi times the diameter",["diameter plus radius","radius minus diameter","half of the length"],"C = πd.")
H("815.d.2.A",3,"A student flips through a machinist's handbook for drill sizes and thread tables, and reads a tool maker's catalog for insert grades. She is analyzing:","resources such as The Machinery's Handbook and manufacturers' catalogs",["a novel","a comic book","a menu"],"Reference works give shop data.")
H("815.d.2.A",4,"A machinist's handbook is a go-to resource for:","tables of dimensions, speeds, feeds and formulas",["recipes","sports statistics","travel routes"],"Handbooks hold shop data.")
H("815.d.2.B",3,"A class compares how a coated stick electrode and a continuously fed wire electrode create a weld, noting shielding and ease of use. They are examining the theory of:","shielded metal arc welding and gas metal arc welding",["gas heating of food","laser printing","soldering circuits"],"SMAW and GMAW differ in electrode and shielding.")
H("815.d.2.B",4,"In gas metal arc welding, the weld pool is protected from air by:","a shielding gas",["a cloth","a coat of paint","water only"],"Shielding gas protects the pool.")
H("815.d.2.C",3,"A tour of a local shop shows workers cutting, bending and joining thin sheets into ductwork and cabinets. The class is examining:","the sheet metal industry",["the textile industry","the lumber industry","the food industry"],"Sheet metal makes ducts and enclosures.")
H("815.d.2.C",4,"A typical product made in a sheet metal shop is:","heating and cooling ductwork",["bread","clothing","paper"],"Ducts are sheet metal products.")
H("815.d.2.D",3,"A student reads the markings on a grinding wheel to learn the abrasive type, grit size and bond, and checks the maximum safe speed printed on it. She is examining:","the nomenclature of abrasive wheels",["the structure of cloth","the parts of a bicycle","the keys of a piano"],"Wheel markings convey type and limits.")
H("815.d.2.D",4,"The maximum operating speed marked on a grinding wheel should:","never be exceeded",["be ignored","be doubled","be guessed"],"Overspeed can burst a wheel.")
H("815.d.3.A",3,"A student uses a hacksaw, a hand file, a bench grinder and a portable drill to cut and finish a steel bracket. She is using:","hand and power tools and equipment commonly employed in metal manufacturing",["only kitchen tools","only paint brushes","only garden tools"],"Hand and power tools shape metal.")
H("815.d.3.A",4,"Before using a power grinder, a worker should wear:","eye and face protection",["flip-flops","a loose scarf","earbuds"],"Eye protection is required.")
H("815.d.3.B",3,"Used cutting oil and metal-contaminated rags are placed in labeled containers and picked up by a licensed hauler rather than being thrown away. This shows how to:","dispose of environmentally hazardous materials used in metal manufacturing",["hide waste","pour waste down a drain","bury waste"],"Hazardous waste needs proper disposal.")
H("815.d.3.B",4,"Used cutting fluid should be disposed of:","through an approved hazardous waste process",["down a floor drain","on the ground","in the trash"],"Follow environmental rules.")
H("815.d.4.A",3,"Before turning a shaft, a student chucks it in a three-jaw chuck, and before milling a plate she clamps it in a vise with parallels. They are using:","work mounting procedures on appropriate machines",["only paint","only tape","only glue"],"Proper mounting holds work securely.")
H("815.d.4.A",4,"A reason to use parallels under a part in a mill vise is to:","seat the part evenly and keep it level",["make the part weaker","hide the part","speed the spindle"],"Parallels support and level work.")
H("815.d.4.B",3,"A class rotates among a drill press, a lathe, a band saw, a surface grinder and a mill, each performing a short job on every machine. They are operating:","machine tools such as the drill press, lathe, saw, grinder and mill",["only kitchen appliances","only sewing machines","only printers"],"Machine tools shape workpieces.")
H("815.d.4.B",4,"A drill press is mostly used to:","drill accurate holes",["turn long shafts","weld seams","bend sheets"],"Drill presses make holes.")
H("815.d.4.C",3,"A student sets the lathe to cut a 1/2-13 thread on a bolt blank, turns a gentle taper on a handle and drills a center hole. She is executing:","lathe procedures such as cutting threads, turning tapers and drilling",["only sanding","only painting","only sawing"],"Lathes cut threads and tapers.")
H("815.d.4.C",4,"A taper turned on a lathe is a feature whose diameter:","gradually changes along its length",["stays the same","jumps abruptly","is zero"],"Tapers narrow gradually.")
H("815.d.4.D",3,"A student uses a vertical mill to face a block flat, cut a 45-degree bevel and mill a small groove. She is executing:","milling procedures such as milling flat surfaces, bevels, chamfers and grooves",["lathe procedures only","welding only","painting only"],"Mills shape flat features.")
H("815.d.4.D",4,"A chamfer is a feature that:","angles or slopes an edge",["is a hole","is a thread","is a weld"],"Chamfers bevel edges.")
H("815.d.5.A",3,"A student makes a straight cut with a band saw, a bevel cut with a torch and a hole with a plasma cutter in a steel plate. She is performing:","cutting processes such as straight cuts, bevel cuts and hole cutting",["only sanding","only polishing","only taping"],"Cutting processes separate metal.")
H("815.d.5.A",4,"Before cutting with an oxy-fuel torch, the area should be:","cleared of flammable materials",["covered with paper","sprayed with solvent","left cluttered"],"Fire safety first.")
H("815.d.5.B",3,"A beginner welder selects an E6013 stick electrode for a mild steel lap joint and strikes an arc on a practice plate. She is using:","the common types of electrodes with shielded metal arc welding",["only a soldering iron","only a glue gun","only a stapler"],"Choose electrodes for the job.")
H("815.d.5.B",4,"In stick welding, the flux coating on the electrode:","produces a shielding gas and slag",["adds color","cools the arc","dries the rod"],"Flux shields the weld pool.")
H("815.d.5.C",3,"A student welds a flat plate, then a horizontal joint and then a vertical joint with a wire-feed welder. She is practicing gas metal arc welding in:","multiple positions",["only one position","no position","a random mix of tools"],"Positions test skill.")
H("815.d.5.C",4,"Welding positions include flat, horizontal, vertical and:","overhead",["underwater only","upside-down only","none"],"Four basic positions.")
H("815.d.5.D",3,"An inspector gauges a fillet weld's leg size, looks for undercut and compares the results with the American Welding Society code. She is inspecting:","groove and fillet welds to AWS and other codes",["paint finishes","clothing seams","wood joints"],"Welds are inspected to codes.")
H("815.d.5.D",4,"A visual weld inspection looks for defects such as:","cracks, porosity and undercut",["bright colors","brand names","hair"],"Visible defects.")
H("815.d.6.A",3,"A machinist calculates the center-to-center spacing of three holes using a formula and checks it with a caliper. He is using:","mathematics in precision measuring operations",["art techniques","music scales","cooking times"],"Math supports precision.")
H("815.d.6.A",4,"Three holes are spaced 2.5 inches apart. The distance from the first to the third is:","5.0 inches",["2.5 inches","7.5 inches","1.25 inches"],"2 spaces × 2.5 = 5.0.")
H("815.d.6.B",3,"A student reads a print for a machined plate showing a top view, a section view and a table of hole sizes. She is interpreting:","engineering drawings, charts and diagrams related to the process",["a bus schedule","a menu","a map of a park"],"Prints guide machining.")
H("815.d.6.B",4,"A section view on a drawing is used to show:","the inside features of a part",["the painting plan","the shipping label","the customer"],"Sections reveal interiors.")
H("815.d.7.A",3,"A student compares galvanized steel, aluminum and stainless sheet in different gauges and notes how each bends and resists rust. She is analyzing:","the types, sizes and properties of sheet metal materials",["types of paper","sizes of shoes","properties of cloth"],"Sheet materials differ.")
H("815.d.7.A",4,"A higher gauge number for sheet metal means that the sheet is:","thinner",["thicker","longer","wider"],"Gauge numbers run opposite to thickness.")
H("815.d.7.B",3,"A class learns how acetylene and oxygen burn to produce a cutting flame and how to use a neutral flame on thin sheet. They are analyzing:","the fundamentals of oxy-fuel processes as related to sheet metal",["the fundamentals of baking","the fundamentals of painting","the fundamentals of sewing"],"Oxy-fuel uses fuel gas and oxygen.")
H("815.d.7.B",4,"An oxy-fuel torch burns:","a fuel gas with oxygen",["only water","only air","only oil"],"Fuel plus oxygen.")
H("815.d.7.C",3,"A student compares using a stick electrode and a wire-feed gun on thin sheet to see which controls heat and burn-through better. She is analyzing the fundamentals of:","shielded metal arc and gas metal arc welding for sheet metal",["gluing paper","stitching cloth","sanding wood"],"Heat control matters on thin metal.")
H("815.d.7.C",4,"On thin sheet metal, a welder must be careful to avoid:","burn-through from too much heat",["too little air","a lack of glare","extra color"],"Thin metal burns through.")
H("815.d.8.A",3,"A student checks that guards are on the shear and brake, keeps fingers clear of the blade and wears gloves when handling sharp edges. She is practicing:","safe use of equipment",["careless handling","random handling","rushed work"],"Sharp edges and shears are hazards.")
H("815.d.8.A",4,"When handling sheet metal, a worker should wear gloves to:","protect hands from sharp edges",["keep hands clean","show style","hold heat"],"Sheet edges cut.")
H("815.d.8.B",3,"Scrap flux, solvent-soaked wipes and spent cleaning chemicals from the sheet metal area are placed in approved waste containers. This shows how to:","dispose of hazardous materials used in sheet metal manufacturing",["hide waste","ignore waste","burn waste"],"Proper disposal.")
H("815.d.8.B",4,"Solvent-soaked rags from a sheet metal shop should be stored in:","a labeled, closed metal container",["a cardboard box","an open trash can","a plastic bag"],"Closed metal cans reduce fire risk.")
H("815.d.9.A",3,"A student sketches a flat pattern for a box with tabs and bend lines on a sheet before cutting. She is drawing:","simple metal layouts",["a portrait","a cartoon","a poster"],"Layouts plan the cuts and bends.")
H("815.d.9.A",4,"Bend lines on a flat pattern show:","where the metal will be folded",["where to paint","where to weld","where to ship"],"Bend lines mark folds.")
H("815.d.9.B",3,"A class folds the edges of two sheets, hooks them together and flattens the joint to create a strong lap. They are constructing:","common sheet metal seams",["fabric hems","paper folds","wood joints"],"Seams join sheets.")
H("815.d.9.B",4,"A common seam used to join sheet metal pieces without welding is a:","Pittsburgh lock seam",["glue seam","paper seam","string seam"],"Pittsburgh lock is a common HVAC seam.")

# ---- 816 Metal Fabrication and Machining II ----
H("816.d.1.A",3,"Three fitters on a pipe rack job find the pipe lengths do not match the print, so they compare notes, recheck the measurements together and agree on a fix. They are using:","teamwork to solve problems",["working alone in silence","blaming each other","waiting for the shift to end"],"Teams solve problems by pooling information.")
H("816.d.1.A",4,"On a fabrication crew, the best response to a disagreement about a cut list is to:","check the print together and agree on the correct dimension",["let the loudest person decide","cut both versions","skip the part"],"Facts from the print settle it.")
H("816.d.1.B",3,"On a weld crew, one worker schedules tasks and reports to the supervisor while the others carry out the assigned welds. These are examples of:","team roles such as leader and member",["unrelated hobbies","personal nicknames","break times"],"Leaders coordinate; members execute.")
H("816.d.1.B",4,"A team leader in a fabrication shop is mainly responsible for:","coordinating the work and keeping the team on schedule",["doing every task alone","avoiding communication","ignoring deadlines"],"Leaders organize and guide.")
H("816.d.1.C",3,"A shop owner hires and promotes workers based on skills and does not treat applicants differently because of their race, sex or religion. This follows:","Equal Employment Opportunity law",["a pricing rule","a paint code","a shipping rule"],"EEO law bars job discrimination.")
H("816.d.1.C",4,"Equal Employment Opportunity laws protect workers from:","discrimination in hiring and on the job",["all deadlines","bad weather","machine noise"],"They prohibit discrimination.")
H("816.d.1.D",3,"A machinist lists the day's jobs in order of due date, estimates how long each takes and blocks out time for setups and breaks. She is using:","time-management techniques to develop a work schedule",["random guesses","a coin flip","avoidance"],"Scheduling allocates time to tasks.")
H("816.d.1.D",4,"The best way to schedule jobs with different due dates is to:","order them by deadline and estimated time",["start with the easiest","start with the longest","pick at random"],"Deadlines drive priority.")
H("816.d.2.A",3,"A fabricator explains a delay to a customer by phone, writes a clear note to the night shift and asks a vendor for a quote in an email. She is demonstrating:","effective communication with people from varied backgrounds",["silent work","mumbling","gossip"],"Different audiences need clear messages.")
H("816.d.2.A",4,"When handing a job off to the next shift, a worker should leave:","a clear written note on status and next steps",["no information","a vague hint","only a smile"],"Written notes prevent errors.")
H("816.d.2.B",3,"A supervisor hands a technician a work order listing the part number, quantity, material and due date. The technician checks off each step as it is finished. This shows how to:","successfully complete work orders",["lose paperwork","skip steps","guess the quantity"],"Work orders track tasks.")
H("816.d.2.B",4,"A work order for a batch of brackets usually lists:","the part number, quantity and due date",["the worker's hobbies","the weather forecast","the lunch menu"],"It defines the job.")
H("816.d.2.C",3,"A shop estimates a welding job at 12 hours and charges $45 per hour for labor. To find the labor cost, the estimator uses the formula:","labor cost = hours × hourly rate",["labor cost = hours + rate","labor cost = rate ÷ hours","labor cost = hours − rate"],"Cost is hours times rate.")
H("816.d.2.C",4,"A job takes 12 hours of labor at $45 per hour. The labor cost is:","$540",["$57","$480","$600"],"12 × 45 = 540.")
H("816.d.2.D",3,"A welder reads a detailed fabrication print with a section view, weld symbols, a bill of materials and tolerance notes before assembling a frame. She is interpreting:","advanced engineering drawings, charts, diagrams and welding symbols",["a comic strip","a bus schedule","a recipe"],"Advanced prints guide assembly.")
H("816.d.2.D",4,"A bill of materials on a fabrication print lists:","the parts and quantities needed",["the shop's profits","the workers' names","the customer's address"],"BOM lists parts.")
H("816.d.2.E",3,"A machinist uses algebra to find the diameter of a hole that is 0.050 inch larger than a 0.625-inch pin. He is demonstrating:","calculation for precision measuring operations using algebra",["rough sketching","paint mixing","guessing"],"Algebra supports precise sizing.")
H("816.d.2.E",4,"A hole must be 0.050 inch larger than a 0.625-inch pin. The hole diameter is:","0.675 inch",["0.575 inch","0.725 inch","0.600 inch"],"0.625 + 0.050 = 0.675.")
H("816.d.3.A",3,"A student checks welding code books, material specification sheets and a safety data sheet before choosing a filler metal. She is analyzing:","resources found in various manufacturing reference materials",["a novel","a sports page","a game guide"],"Reference materials give authoritative data.")
H("816.d.3.A",4,"A material specification sheet tells a fabricator the metal's:","grade, strength and composition",["owner's name","delivery driver","paint trend"],"Specs describe the material.")
H("816.d.3.B",3,"A student shows how a plasma torch, a TIG torch and a wire-feed gun each form a joint and where each is best used. She is demonstrating knowledge of:","the various welding processes",["baking methods","painting methods","sewing methods"],"Different processes suit different jobs.")
H("816.d.3.B",4,"Gas tungsten arc welding (TIG) is often chosen for:","clean, precise welds on thin material",["rough outdoor repairs only","cutting wood","driving nails"],"TIG gives control.")
H("816.d.3.C",3,"A class visits a sheet metal contractor and sees how drawings turn into flat patterns, then into folded and seamed ducts. They are examining:","the sheet metal industry",["the dairy industry","the movie industry","the fishing industry"],"Sheet metal trades make ducts and enclosures.")
H("816.d.3.C",4,"One career in the sheet metal industry is a:","duct fabricator",["pastry chef","pilot","lifeguard"],"Fabricators build ducts.")
H("816.d.3.D",3,"A student compares aluminum oxide, silicon carbide and ceramic grinding wheels for a stainless job and a hardened tool steel job. She is examining:","the advanced use of abrasives",["watercolor paints","yarns","wood stains"],"Abrasive choice depends on material.")
H("816.d.3.D",4,"A grinding wheel with a higher grit number is:","finer and leaves a smoother finish",["coarser and rougher","wider","heavier"],"Higher grit means finer.")
H("816.d.4.A",3,"A student sets up a MIG welder, a plasma cutter and a surface grinder and runs a short job on each under supervision. She is operating:","various welding machines, cutting equipment and grinding equipment",["a printer","a microwave","a camera"],"Hands-on machine operation.")
H("816.d.4.A",4,"Before striking an arc on a welding machine, a welder checks:","that the ground clamp is attached and the area is clear of flammables",["the TV schedule","the clock only","the music playlist"],"Ground and fire safety.")
H("816.d.4.B",3,"A class watches a CNC machine cut a program of holes and slots automatically after a student loads the code and sets the work zero. They are demonstrating knowledge of:","computer numerical control (CNC) machines",["hand cranks only","kitchen mixers","bicycle pedals"],"CNC follows coded instructions.")
H("816.d.4.B",4,"A CNC machine moves its tool according to:","a coded program",["the operator's mood","random vibration","the room light"],"Programmed instructions control motion.")
H("816.d.4.C",3,"A robotic welding cell repeats the same weld path on every frame while a human loads the parts. The class is demonstrating knowledge of:","automated welding machines",["manual hammering only","hand painting","hand sewing"],"Automation repeats welds consistently.")
H("816.d.4.C",4,"A key benefit of an automated welding cell is:","consistent weld quality on repetitive jobs",["slower output","random weld paths","no setup needed"],"Repeatability.")
H("816.d.4.D",3,"A student researches laser cutting, additive manufacturing and collaborative robots to see how they could change a metal shop in the next ten years. She is demonstrating knowledge of:","emerging technologies that may affect metal manufacturing",["old tools only","traditional crafts only","weather"],"New technology changes industry.")
H("816.d.4.D",4,"A 3D metal printer builds a part by:","adding material layer by layer",["cutting only","pouring into a mold only","bending only"],"Additive manufacturing.")
H("816.d.4.E",3,"A shop sends used coolant, spent solvents and contaminated filters to a certified waste hauler instead of the dumpster. This shows how to:","dispose of environmentally hazardous materials associated with the equipment",["hide waste","dump waste","burn waste"],"Proper disposal of hazardous waste.")
H("816.d.4.E",4,"Information on the hazards of a cutting fluid and how to dispose of it is found on its:","safety data sheet (SDS)",["price tag","shipping label","brand logo"],"SDS gives hazard and disposal info.")
H("816.d.5.A",3,"A student clamps round stock in a collet, secures a block in a vise and bolts a casting to a rotary table. She is using:","work mounting procedures on appropriate machines",["only tape","only glue","only magnets for everything"],"Proper holding for each part.")
H("816.d.5.A",4,"A rigid setup matters when holding work because a loose part can:","move or fly out during cutting",["cut faster","look nicer","cool the tool"],"Work-holding safety.")
H("816.d.5.B",3,"A class compares drilling, turning, sawing and grinding to decide which operation removes the most stock and which gives the smoothest finish. They are examining:","cutting operations such as drill press, lathe, saw and grinder",["sweeping operations","painting operations","filing operations"],"Different operations suit different goals.")
H("816.d.5.B",4,"Of these, the operation that usually gives the smoothest surface finish is:","grinding",["sawing","rough drilling","flame cutting"],"Grinding refines surfaces.")
H("816.d.5.C",3,"A student cuts external threads on a shaft, turns a 3-degree taper and uses a reamer to finish a hole to size on the lathe. She is executing:","lathe procedures such as cutting threads, turning tapers, drilling and reaming",["welding steps only","sewing steps only","painting steps only"],"Lathe operations.")
H("816.d.5.C",4,"A reamer is used to:","finish a drilled hole to an accurate size",["cut external threads","bend a bar","weld a joint"],"Reamers refine holes.")
H("816.d.5.D",3,"A student mills the top of a block flat, cuts a 45-degree bevel on its edge, adds a chamfer and mills a keyseat in a shaft. She is milling:","flat surfaces, bevels, chamfers, grooves and key-seats",["fabric swatches","wood planks only","glass panes"],"Mill features.")
H("816.d.5.D",4,"A keyseat is a slot cut in a shaft to hold:","a key that locks a pulley or gear in place",["a battery","a light bulb","a nail"],"Keys transmit torque.")
H("816.d.5.E",3,"A student turns a shaft to within 0.001 inch of the print and mills a plate with flatness held to a few thousandths. She is machining:","precision pieces",["rough pieces only","decorative pieces only","paper pieces"],"Precision work meets tolerances.")
H("816.d.5.E",4,"A precision part is one that is made:","to tight tolerances that meet the print",["as fast as possible regardless of size","without measuring","in any size"],"Tolerance defines precision.")
H("816.d.6.A",3,"A student cuts a plate with an oxy-fuel torch and then with a plasma cutter and compares the edge quality. She is demonstrating:","cutting processes such as oxy-fuel and plasma",["sewing processes","baking processes","printing processes"],"Different cutting processes.")
H("816.d.6.A",4,"A plasma cutter is able to cut:","any electrically conductive metal",["only wood","only plastic","only glass"],"Plasma needs conductive metal.")
H("816.d.6.B",3,"A student picks an E7018 low-hydrogen electrode for a structural joint, sets the amperage and lays a bead with the stick welder. She is demonstrating:","the use of common electrodes with shielded metal arc welding",["glue use","hand soldering","taping"],"Electrodes are chosen for the application.")
H("816.d.6.B",4,"The numbers in an electrode designation such as E7018 indicate its:","tensile strength and usage characteristics",["price","color","length only"],"E70 = 70,000 psi.")
H("816.d.6.C",3,"A student welds a joint with a stick welder, a wire-feed welder and a TIG torch, adjusting settings for each process. She is using:","shielded metal arc, gas metal arc and gas tungsten arc welding",["only a soldering iron","only a glue gun","only a stapler"],"Three common arc processes.")
H("816.d.6.C",4,"Of the three, the process that uses a non-consumable tungsten electrode is:","gas tungsten arc welding",["stick welding","wire-feed welding","soldering"],"TIG uses tungsten.")
H("816.d.6.D",3,"An inspector checks completed welds for size, cracks, porosity and fusion against the American Welding Society and Canadian Welding Bureau codes. She is inspecting:","welds to AWS, CWB and other codes",["paintwork","tile work","woodwork"],"Codes define acceptable welds.")
H("816.d.6.D",4,"Porosity in a weld looks like:","small gas holes in the weld metal",["a bright color","a smooth shine","tight ripples"],"Gas pockets form pores.")
H("816.d.7.A",3,"A shop estimates a sheet metal job at 20 hours of labor and a $38 hourly rate. She is estimating:","labor costs",["lunch costs","weather costs","paint colors"],"Labor = hours × rate.")
H("816.d.7.A",4,"Twenty hours at $38 per hour gives a labor cost of:","$760",["$58","$680","$800"],"20 × 38 = 760.")
H("816.d.7.B",3,"A machinist uses trigonometry to find the length of a hypotenuse for an angled brace and checks it with a gauge. He is using:","advanced mathematics in precision measuring operations",["art projects","guesses","stories"],"Trig supports precision.")
H("816.d.7.B",4,"A brace forms a right triangle with legs of 3 inches and 4 inches. The length of the brace is:","5 inches",["7 inches","6 inches","12 inches"],"3-4-5.")
H("816.d.7.C",3,"A fabricator reads an industrial blueprint with title block, revision table, hidden lines and a notes section before cutting steel. She is interpreting:","industrial standard blueprints, drawings, charts and diagrams",["a map of a mall","a lunch menu","a train schedule"],"Blueprints guide fabrication.")
H("816.d.7.C",4,"The revision table on a blueprint tells the reader:","what has changed since earlier versions",["the price","the weather","the shop's address"],"Revisions track changes.")
H("816.d.8.A",3,"A student compares galvanized steel, stainless, aluminum and the rivets, bolts and screws used to join them. She is analyzing:","properties of sheet metal materials and fasteners",["fabric swatches","paper types","wood grains"],"Materials and fasteners.")
H("816.d.8.A",4,"A reason to use stainless sheet instead of plain steel in a food kitchen is that stainless:","resists rust and is easy to clean",["rusts quickly","is paper thin","is soft like wax"],"Corrosion resistance.")
H("816.d.8.B",3,"A class studies how an oxygen-acetylene cutting torch can trim sheet metal and what flame settings protect thin material. They are analyzing:","oxy-fuel processes as related to sheet metal",["inkjet processes","sewing processes","baking processes"],"Oxy-fuel on sheet.")
H("816.d.8.B",4,"To avoid warping thin sheet while cutting with a torch, a worker should:","use a small tip and travel quickly",["use the largest tip","stop and stay","remove all shielding"],"Less heat input.")
H("816.d.8.C",3,"A class compares stick, wire-feed and TIG methods for joining sheet metal and explains which gives the least distortion. They are demonstrating knowledge of:","shielded metal arc, gas metal arc and gas tungsten arc welding for sheet metal",["glue types","yarn types","paint types"],"Welding choices for sheet.")
H("816.d.8.C",4,"For welding very thin sheet with minimal distortion, a good choice is:","gas tungsten arc welding with low heat",["a heavy stick electrode","a torch cut","a hammer weld"],"TIG gives precise heat control.")
H("816.d.9.A",3,"Before running the box and pan brake, a student checks the guards, keeps hands clear of the clamping area and wears gloves. She is:","using equipment commonly employed in sheet metal safely",["working carelessly","ignoring rules","rushing"],"Safe operation of sheet metal equipment.")
H("816.d.9.A",4,"A power shear should be operated only:","with guards in place and hands clear of the blade",["with the guard removed","while talking on a phone","without training"],"Guards prevent injuries.")
H("816.d.9.B",3,"A shop collects used solder flux, spent solvents and oily rags from the sheet metal area in approved waste containers. This shows how to:","dispose of environmentally hazardous materials used in sheet metal manufacturing",["hide waste","dump waste","burn waste"],"Environmental compliance.")
H("816.d.9.B",4,"Used solvent from cleaning sheet metal should be:","collected in a labeled container for proper disposal",["poured in the sink","poured outdoors","thrown in trash"],"Hazardous waste.")
H("816.d.9.C",3,"A student researches how laser cutters, automatic bending machines and robot arms are changing sheet metal shops. She is demonstrating knowledge of:","emerging technologies that may affect sheet metal manufacturing",["old craft methods only","cooking","painting"],"New technology in sheet metal.")
H("816.d.9.C",4,"A fiber laser cutter can cut sheet metal:","quickly and precisely from a digital file",["only by hand","only by chance","only with a hammer"],"Automation increases precision.")
H("816.d.10.A",3,"A student draws a flat pattern for a rectangular-to-round transition using parallel line and triangulation methods. She is drawing:","advanced sheet metal layouts",["a doodle","a portrait","a cartoon"],"Advanced layouts use triangulation.")
H("816.d.10.A",4,"Triangulation is a pattern development method used for:","transition fittings between different shapes",["simple flat boxes only","round cans only","nothing"],"Transitions are triangulated.")
H("816.d.10.B",3,"A class forms a grooved seam, a standing seam and a Pittsburgh lock on practice sheets and compares their strength. They are constructing:","sheet metal seams",["fabric seams","paper creases","wood joints"],"Different seams for different uses.")
H("816.d.10.B",4,"A standing seam is commonly used on:","metal roofing",["bicycles","paper cups","T-shirts"],"Standing seam roofs.")
H("816.d.10.C",3,"A student lays out and builds a fitting that changes a duct from square to round and another that jogs the duct sideways around a beam. She is constructing:","transitions and offsets",["pillows","birdhouses","paper planes"],"Duct fittings.")
H("816.d.10.C",4,"An offset fitting in a duct run is used to:","shift the duct around an obstruction",["add color","block air","increase noise"],"Offsets bypass obstacles.")
H("816.d.10.D",3,"A student uses a TIG torch with a filler rod to join thin stainless panels into a small box without warping them. She is using:","the gas tungsten arc welding process in sheet metal construction",["a stapler","a glue gun","a hot iron"],"TIG on sheet.")
H("816.d.10.D",4,"A shielding gas commonly used with TIG welding is:","argon",["oxygen only","hydrogen only","air only"],"Argon shields the weld.")
H("816.d.10.E",3,"A student applies layout, cutting, forming, seaming and welding to build a ventilation hood from sheet steel to a print. She is applying:","the principles of sheet metal construction to fabrication of a project",["a drawing contest","a puzzle","a cooking recipe"],"Combine skills for a project.")
H("816.d.10.E",4,"When building a project from a print, a fabricator should always:","verify dimensions against the drawing before cutting",["cut first","guess dimensions","skip the print"],"Check before you cut.")
H("816.d.10.F",3,"A student logs her sheet metal projects and skills in a portfolio and practices them on a job-shadow day at a local shop. She is applying:","sheet metal skills to career preparation learning experiences",["gaming skills only","sports skills only","cooking skills only"],"Skills are practiced in career settings.")
H("816.d.10.F",4,"A portfolio of completed fabrication projects helps a job applicant by:","showing employers what skills she has",["hiding her abilities","replacing a resume","avoiding interviews"],"Evidence of skill.")

